The core difference between ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) and ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) is simply a matter 
of where the heavy lifting happens: inside a dedicated middleman server, or directly inside the final destination database.

Technical Breakdown :-

ETL (The Traditional Way): Data leaves the source and lands in a separate, 
dedicated transformation engine (like an Informatica server or an on-premise Hadoop cluster). 
It gets cleaned, joined, and aggregated there. Only the final, polished data is written into the Data Warehouse.

ELT (The Modern Way): Data is extracted and loaded immediately into the target Cloud Data Warehouse 
(like Snowflake or BigQuery) or Lakehouse in its rawest, messiest form. 
The transformation happens afterward, executing directly inside the warehouse using its own massive compute power.

Buisness Context :-
This shift was entirely driven by the economics of the cloud. Historically, 
legacy on-premise data warehouses had highly constrained compute and expensive storage; 
you couldn't afford to waste their cycles cleaning messy data. Today, cloud storage is practically free,
and cloud compute is infinitely scalable. It makes business sense to ingest all raw data instantly,
ensuring no data is ever dropped, and transform it on-demand.

Design Decision :- 
As a modern data engineer, I almost exclusively build ELT architectures,
and I strictly decouple the 'EL' from the 'T'. I use managed services like Fivetran or Airbyte to mindlessly extract
and load raw data into a 'Bronze' layer. Then, I use a framework like dbt (data build tool) to handle the 'T'. 
dbt executes modular, version-controlled SQL directly against the warehouse to transform that raw data into 'Silver' 
and 'Gold' models. This eliminates the need to maintain fragile, custom Python transformation servers in the middle 
of our pipeline.
